ut this BMW still has  plenty of power under the hood it's  straight six-cylinder engine has an  output of 135 kilowatts that enables the  car to reach a top speed of 200  kilometers an hour even in its heyday  the BMW 730 was a luxury model with a  price tag of over 40,000 Deutsche marks  only top earners could afford one for  many the metallic amazonite green paint  takes a bit of getting used to but for  Daniel it had to be this color  aside from the fact that greens his  favorite color he says his parents had a  sofa in their living room in the 1980s  that was almost exactly this shade of  fern green so you could say it was love  at first sight  the color combination of green exterior  and green interior is unique from  today's standpoint but in the 70s these  colors were invoked though by the late  1970s they were out again he figures  this color combo really is one of a kind  I'm honest these days Daniel Durham on  only drives his BMW 730 for fun a  longtime member of their line EFT  antique car club he takes part and day  trips and weekend excursions then his  green car always turns heads although  many people buy classic cars as an  investment for Daniel it's never been  about the money  he feels his car itself is a treasure on  the classic car market 7 Series models  lacked the status of ones like the  Mercedes s-class these cars are bought  by people who love them they'll retain  their value if well cared for but won't  shoot up in price like vintage cars have  of late but for him that's immaterial  it's all about this car and it's  priceless  compared to other classic cars demand  for first generation BMW 7-series models  is relatively low so prices remain  moderate but no matter what its market  value Daniel wouldn't trade in his green  BMW for anything
 

